    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "We document the evolution of damage zones associated with strike-slip faults in the Valley of Fire State Park in SE Nevada, from detailed field and statistical study of faults with increasing slip magnitude.\r\n\r\nThis work envisions that faults initiate as sheared joints with discontinuous pockets of damage zone localized at fault tips and fault surface irregularities. When faults develop by segment linkage with increasing slip (slip>5m), their damage zone becomes longer and wider by progressive infilling and is organized into two distinct components with different geometrical and statistical characteristics. The inner damage zone flanks the fault core and has a relatively high fracture frequency and a relatively constant thickness. The outer damage zone is adjacent to the inner damage zone and has a lower fracture frequency than the inner zone and a more variable thickness. These two components form progressively when faults develop, as proposed in a new conceptual model for damage zone evolution with fault growth.\r\n\r\nResults from scanline surveys show that fault damage zones have statistical properties that evolve with fault growth and can be correlated with different stages of the development of the fault networks.\r\n\r\nThe conceptual model and statistical results in this work may be used to better estimate the geometrical and statistical properties of damage zones associated with faults with resolvable offset in the subsurface.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
